---
/** Method sheet — Common Ground (the nightly trending ledger, /trending).
 *  Same six sections as every other method sheet on this site (MethodenblattPattern.astro is
 *  the pattern). The source table is read from the newest committed day, so a source that is
 *  added, renamed or relicensed in the pipeline shows up here without anyone editing prose. */
import TableFallback from '@/components/dataviz/TableFallback.astro'
import { getLatestTrending } from '@/lib/trending/data'
import { fmtDateLong, platformLabel } from '@/lib/trending/format'

const day = getLatestTrending()
const sourceRows = (day?.sources ?? []).map((s) => ({
  source: platformLabel(s.id),
  what: s.name,
  status: s.status === 'ok' ? 'ok' : `${s.status}${s.note ? ` — ${s.note}` : ''}`,
  signals: s.count,
  as_of: s.as_of ?? '—',
  licence: s.licence,
}))

const limits = [
  'The audience counts are sampled at the edge, not a census: Cloudflare returns them with an average sample interval, which is committed beside the numbers so a reader can see what a count is made of.',
  'Every audience class rests on a self-declaration. A user agent can say anything; the classification believes the string, so a crawler that disguises itself is counted as whatever it claims to be.',
  'The novelty reading — first seen, days hot — only becomes meaningful once the archive has fourteen days in it. Before that, a topic looks new because the archive is new.',
  'A crossing is a coincidence of labels, not a claim about intent, influence or causality. Two platforms carrying the same words on the same morning is exactly that, and nothing more.',
  'The sources lean English, with some German through the fediverse instance that is read; a topic large in another language can be entirely absent here without being absent from the web.',
  'Sources fail. A source that does not answer is recorded as unavailable with the reason, and the day is published with fewer of them — never backfilled, never quietly replaced by a neighbour.',
]
---

<main id="main" class="flex-1">
  <div class="trending-method mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-14">
    <h1 class="mb-8 text-xl font-semibold">Method sheet — Common Ground</h1>

    <section class="mb-10 border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5">
      <h2 class="mb-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">What this is</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed">
        A ledger, not a summary. Every morning the pipeline reads what a set of independent, open sources declare
        they are trending, keeps each label and count exactly as its source states it, and then asks one question
        of the whole set: what is more than one of them looking at? The crossing is a written rule over tokens, so
        anyone can run it again on the committed file and get the same list. Nothing here is summarised, ranked or
        phrased by a language model.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">1. Sources &amp; licences</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        {day
          ? `The sources of the newest committed day (${fmtDateLong(day.date)}), with what each one returned that morning. Only labels, links and the sources' own counts are kept — never a body text, never a user.`
          : 'No day has been committed yet; the table fills itself from the newest committed file.'}
      </p>
      {sourceRows.length > 0 && (
        <div class="mt-3 text-sm">
          <TableFallback
            summary="The sources, as a table"
            open={true}
            columns={[
              { key: 'source', label: 'Source', nowrap: true },
              { key: 'what', label: 'What is read' },
              { key: 'status', label: 'Status' },
              { key: 'signals', label: 'Signals', align: 'right' },
              { key: 'as_of', label: 'As of', nowrap: true },
              { key: 'licence', label: 'Licence / terms' },
            ]}
            rows={sourceRows}
            caption="The sources of the newest committed trending day, with status, signal count, as-of date and licence"
            provenance="Read from the committed day file, not from a list kept by hand: a source added or relicensed in the pipeline changes this table on the next build."
          />
        </div>
      )}
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">2. Cadence</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        One run per day, at 06:40 UTC, committed as a dated JSON file that is never edited afterwards. One source
        is deliberately out of step: Wikipedia publishes a day&rsquo;s most-read articles only after that day has
        closed, so the reader takes the previous day and looks back up to four days for the newest complete list —
        which is why the Wikipedia row carries an earlier as-of date than the rest. Who read the page is counted
        one day later still, from Cloudflare&rsquo;s edge analytics: those counts are sampled, and the average
        sample interval is committed beside them as <code class="font-mono text-xs">sample_interval_avg</code> so a
        reader can tell a count from an extrapolation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">3. Processing</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        Each label is reduced to tokens: case folded, diacritics stripped, hashtags split into their words,
        stopwords dropped. Two short labels are treated as the same topic when they share an exact single token,
        when their token sets overlap above the committed Jaccard threshold, or when one set contains the other.
        A short label also attaches to a headline when every one of its tokens occurs in that headline, which is
        how a two-word search trend meets the news story that produced it. Surviving clusters are scored first by
        the number of independent platforms carrying them and then by rank heat within each source, and each
        cluster is shown under the label of its highest-priority source. First-seen and days-hot are read out of
        the last thirty archived days — the memory is the archive itself, and it holds nothing a reader cannot
        open.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        <a href="https://github.com/frankbueltge/frankbueltge.de/tree/main/pipelines/trending"
          class="font-mono text-xs text-fg-faint transition-colors hover:text-fg" rel="noopener">&rarr; pipelines/trending</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">4. Limits of the method</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-fg-muted">{limits.map((i) => <li>{i}</li>)}</ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">5. Compute footprint</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        One run per day: roughly forty HTTP requests against public endpoints, a few seconds of token matching in
        Python, and a commit. No model is called at any point, in the pipeline or on the page, and the site itself
        stays static.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">6. Change log</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-fg-muted">
        <li>2026-09-02 &middot; v1 &middot; first ledger</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <a href="/trending" class="text-sm text-fg-muted transition-colors hover:text-fg">&rarr; To the experiment</a>
  </div>
</main>

<style>
  /* Same table voice as the ledger page; scoped to this sheet (ADR 0010: the primitive ships
     structure, the surface decides how it reads). */
  .trending-method :global(.dv-table-fallback) {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .trending-method :global(.dv-table-fallback > summary) {
    cursor: pointer;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-fg-faint);
  }
  .trending-method :global(.dv-table-fallback > summary:hover) {
    color: var(--color-fg);
  }
  .trending-method :global(.dv-table) {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }
  .trending-method :global(.dv-table th) {
    padding: 0.35rem 0.75rem 0.35rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-fg-muted);
  }
  .trending-method :global(.dv-table td) {
    padding: 0.35rem 0.75rem 0.35rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .trending-method :global(.dv-table [data-align='right']) {
    padding-right: 0;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .trending-method :global(.dv-prov) {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-fg-faint);
  }
</style>
